Get Started Today

</a></p>

<h2><b>What Actually Drives the Cost</b></h2>
<p>Since every property is different, price comes down to a handful of specific factors rather than a flat rate:</p>
<ul><li><b>Material type</b>, a concrete overlay and a stone repolish/reseal use different products and labor, so costs vary between them</li><li><b>Current surface condition</b>, a surface with more wear, staining, or minor damage takes more prep work than one that’s in decent shape</li><li><b>Square footage</b>, larger driveways and patios naturally cost more to resurface than smaller ones</li><li><b>Site accessibility</b>, harder-to-reach areas can add time and labor</li></ul>
<p>This is why getting an accurate number really requires a look at your specific driveway or patio rather than relying on a generic online estimate. For a general sense of what a full new installation runs, our breakdown of<a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-20x20-paver-patio-cost/"> 20×20 paver patio costs</a> is a useful comparison point.</p>
<h2><b>When Resurfacing Makes Sense (vs. Full Replacement)</b></h2>
<p>Resurfacing is a great option for the right situation, but it’s not the answer for every problem. Here’s how we typically think about it:</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Situation</b></td><td><b>Best Option</b></td></tr><tr><td>Surface is worn or fading, but structurally solid</td><td>Resurfacing</td></tr><tr><td>Minor cracking with no major shifting</td><td>Resurfacing</td></tr><tr><td>Working within a tighter budget, not ready for a full rebuild</td><td>Resurfacing</td></tr><tr><td>Major cracking, sinking, or an unstable base</td><td>Full replacement</td></tr><tr><td>Widespread damage across most of the surface</td><td>Full replacement</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The key factor is what’s happening underneath. If the base is solid and the damage is mostly cosmetic, fading, minor surface wear, small cracks, resurfacing can extend the life of your driveway or patio for a fraction of the cost of tearing it out. If the base itself is compromised, resurfacing is just a cosmetic fix over a structural problem, and it won’t hold. This is the same underlying issue we cover in our guide on<a href="/blog/do-professionals-seal-cracks-in-driveways-or-patios/"> whether professionals seal cracks in driveways and patios</a>, where sealing runs into the same base versus surface distinction.</p>
<h2><b>Why Resurfacing Costs Less Than Replacement</b></h2>
<p>The main reason resurfacing is more budget-friendly comes down to what work is actually being skipped. Full replacement means excavation, base rebuilding, and installing an entirely new surface, all labor and material intensive steps. Resurfacing skips all of that and works with the existing structure, which is why it’s often the practical choice for driveways and patios that are showing their age but haven’t failed structurally. When a full rebuild is genuinely the right call, getting the<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> paver base depth</a> correct from the start is what determines whether you’re back in this position again in a few years.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Is resurfacing a good long-term solution, or just a temporary fix?</b></h3>
<p>When the base is solid and the surface damage is cosmetic, resurfacing is a legitimate long-term solution, not just a stopgap. It’s a different situation than patching a crack that keeps coming back.</p>
<h3><b>How do I know if my driveway needs resurfacing or full replacement?</b></h3>
<p>If the surface looks worn or faded but doesn’t have major cracking, sinking, or shifting, resurfacing is usually the right fit. Significant structural issues point to replacement instead.</p>
<h3><b>Does resurfacing work on both concrete and stone/paver surfaces?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, but the process is different for each. Concrete typically gets an overlay coating, while stone and pavers are repolished and resealed rather than coated.</p>
<h3><b>How long does a resurfacing project take compared to full replacement?</b></h3>
<p>Resurfacing is generally faster since it skips excavation and base rebuilding, working instead with the existing structure underneath.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the best way to get an accurate cost for my project?</b></h3>
<p>Since price depends on material, condition, and size, the most reliable way to get an accurate number is a direct assessment of your specific driveway or patio rather than a general online estimate.</p>

<h2><b>Wondering If Resurfacing Is Right for Your Driveway or Patio?</b></h2>
<p>Every property is a little different, which is exactly why we recommend a direct look before settling on a number. Ridgewell Landscape and Design can assess your surface and let you know honestly whether resurfacing or full replacement makes more sense. We serve the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and surrounding communities. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> or<a href="/contact/"> contact us</a> to get started.</p>

